\section{Model}
\label{sec:model}

There are two jurisdictions (local governments) indexed by $i\in\{1,2\}$, and we write $j\neq i$ for the other jurisdiction. Consumers are uniformly distributed on the Hotelling line segment $x\in[0,1]$, and each consumer demands one unit. Throughout, we assume $\alpha\in[0,1]$ and $\rho\in[0,1)$.

\subsection{Downstream actors (downstream firms)}
To avoid confusion with jurisdiction indices, we label the two downstream sellers (downstream firms) by $f\in\{A,B\}$ and write their prices as $(p_A,p_B)$. Jurisdiction $1$ is associated with seller $A$, and jurisdiction $2$ with seller $B$. Marginal cost is constant and equal to $c$ (upstream farmers are competitive, so the supply side is summarized by $c$).

\subsection{Perceived quality and consumer utility (decomposing real vs.~purely persuasive components)}
Jurisdictional public R\&D (cultivar breeding, branding, promotion, etc.) generates a quality component that shifts consumers' \emph{perceived utility}. We refer to this demand shifter as perceived quality $(q_A,q_B)$.

We interpret $q_f$ as \emph{perceived quality}. A fraction $\alpha\in[0,1]$ is
\emph{real quality} that enters welfare, $r_f\equiv \alpha q_f$, while the remainder%
\footnote{While $\alpha$ is introduced here as a reduced-form welfare-relevant share of perceived quality, Section~\ref{sec:welfare} provides a simple two-input extension that microfounds $\alpha$ by decomposing policy into welfare-relevant research and welfare-irrelevant promotion. In that extension, decentralization generates a promotion tilt and lowers the implied welfare-relevant share in closed form, addressing concerns that $\alpha$ is an ad hoc welfare weight.}
$q_f-r_f=(1-\alpha)q_f$ is a \emph{purely persuasive component} that affects demand
but not welfare.
We refer to $\alpha$ as the \emph{real-quality share}.%
\footnote{An alternative welfare criterion, due to \citet{BeckerMurphy1993}, treats advertising as a complementary good that enters consumers' utility directly and is therefore welfare-relevant by construction. Under this view, any demand shift---including pure persuasion---would be welfare-improving, effectively setting $\alpha=1$ for all components. Our approach follows the Dixit--Norman tradition \citep{DixitNorman1978} in which welfare counts only \emph{real} quality improvements. When both perspectives are plausible, one would replace our single $\alpha$ with a weight that interpolates between the two criteria; the qualitative over-/underinvestment threshold would persist but at a different cutoff.}

When consumer $x$ purchases good $f\in\{A,B\}$, her (private) utility is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Ui}
U_f(x)= V + \bar q + q_f - p_f - t\, d_f(x),
\end{equation}
where $t>0$, $d_A(x)=x$, and $d_B(x)=1-x$. We assume $V$ is sufficiently large so that the market is fully covered (every consumer purchases from one of the two sellers).

This decomposition implies that $\alpha=0$ corresponds to a purely persuasive component (pure demand reallocation), whereas $\alpha=1$ corresponds to purely real quality (a genuine welfare improvement).

\begin{equation}\label{eq:decompose}
r_f\equiv \alpha q_f,\qquad q_f-r_f=(1-\alpha)q_f.
\end{equation}

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{(1) Important remark:} Second-stage price competition, demand, and profits are determined by the overall demand shifter $q_f$ that enters consumers' utility. The decomposition in \eqref{eq:decompose} is introduced \emph{solely} to evaluate welfare by counting real quality $r_f$ but excluding the purely persuasive component $q_f-r_f$. Accordingly, the second-stage price equilibrium is expressed in terms of $q_f$ (and the difference $\Delta q$). We treat $\alpha$ as reduced-form in the baseline; Section~\ref{subsec:microfound_alpha} provides a microfoundation by decomposing policy into real and purely persuasive components while preserving the same reduced-form profit dependence on $\Delta q$.

\subsection{R\&D and the production of perceived quality}
Jurisdiction $i\in\{1,2\}$ chooses an R\&D investment level $e_i\ge 0$, which generates perceived quality for the associated seller via
\begin{equation}\label{eq:qi}
q_A = g(e_1) + \rho\, g(e_2),
\qquad
q_B = g(e_2) + \rho\, g(e_1),
\end{equation}
where $g'(e)>0$ and $g''(e)\le 0$ (diminishing marginal returns), and where $\rho\in[0,1)$ captures knowledge spillovers.%
\footnote{We restrict $\rho\ge 0$, treating spillovers as nonnegative knowledge transfers. In practice, a rival's quality improvement may \emph{hurt} a jurisdiction through demand reallocation, which is captured here through the competitive interaction (the $\Delta q$ term in profits) rather than through the sign of $\rho$. Three asymmetry considerations are worth noting: (i)~research spillovers are typically nonnegative and depend on adaptation costs \citep{AlstonNortonPardey1995}; (ii)~promotional spillovers may be negative if a rival's campaign crowds out attention, or positive if it expands category awareness; and (iii)~the symmetric $\rho$ assumption abstracts from geography, absorptive capacity, and institutional heterogeneity. Allowing distinct spillover parameters $\rho_R\neq\rho_M$ is straightforward within our two-input extension (Section~\ref{subsec:microfound_alpha}).}
The R\&D cost is $K(e_i)$, with $K(0)=0$, $K'(e)>0$, and $K''(e)>0$. Finally, for budgetary or administrative reasons, we restrict $e_i\in[0,\bar e]$ for some $\bar e>0$ (the upper bound is nonbinding if $\bar e$ is sufficiently large).


\subsection{Timing and objective functions (a central-government coordination mechanism)}
The timing is as follows.
(0) A central government (coordination mechanism) chooses a matching rate $s$ (allowing $s<0$ captures a tax).
(1) Jurisdictions choose $(e_1,e_2)$.
(2) Downstream actors (sellers $A$ and $B$) simultaneously choose $(p_A,p_B)$.
(3) Consumers choose where to buy.

Jurisdiction $i\in\{1,2\}$'s decentralized objective is to maximize local downstream profit net of the (post-matching) effective R\&D cost,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:local_obj}
W_i^{\mathrm{loc}}=\pi_{f(i)}(p_A,p_B;q_A,q_B) - (1-s)K(e_i),
\qquad f(1)=A,\ f(2)=B,
\end{equation}
with respect to $e_i$.

\medskip
\noindent
\textbf{(2) Admissible set for the matching rate:} We allow $s<0$ (a tax), but we assume at least that the jurisdiction's effective cost share is nonnegative, namely
\begin{equation}\label{eq:s_domain}
s\le 1 \qquad(\text{equivalently, }1-s\ge 0).
\end{equation}
Accordingly, the admissible set is $s\in(-\infty,1]$.
We rule out $s>1$ because it would imply a negative effective cost for the jurisdiction.

